I recently saw [Jacky Zhao's Sunlit](https://jzhao.xyz/thoughts/craft) and appreciate the warmth and sense of relaxation it brings to the page. Much like reading by a sunny window. I wanted to understand how it worked, so I followed the trail: Jacky's implementation traced back to [Sunlit.place](https://www.sunlit.place/) and the [Daylight Computer](https://daylightcomputer.com/), with roots in older [CSS-Tricks dappled light techniques](https://css-tricks.com/css-dappled-light-effect/).

I dug into [the code](https://github.com/jackyzha0/sunlit) and derived a riffed version here: ≈9KB of CSS, one SVG image, zero JavaScript. The effect respects reduced motion preferences, works in all modern browsers, and doesn't block interactions.

Is it a bit silly to add atmospheric lighting effects to a blog? Probably. But it's my blog, and the effect will be fun while it lasts. Hopefully it's so subtle that you've not really noticed it.

Here's how it works  —  and where I took a different approach.

> ## tl;dr
>
> - Built a 2-layer atmospheric effect (dappled leaves + 3D perspective blinds) using CSS gradients and SVG filters
> - Sun angle and opacity animate as you scroll — no JavaScript required, Firefox not supported
> - Total footprint: ≈9KB CSS overhead, minimal SVG, zero JavaScript runtime cost
> - Reduced complexity [CodePen demo](https://codepen.io/khawkins98/pen/ZYQgKOw?editors=1000) to try it yourself

